# Tool input validation

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Issue | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
| Status | Draft |

The registry owns the input boundary for every registered tool so that malformed model calls produce actionable tool results before an executor can act on them.

## Execution contract

- "'`execute` validates required properties, declared types and enum membership against the registered JSON Schema before invoking an executor, returns a `ToolResponse` describing failing paths and constraints without input values on rejection, logs no input values, passes accepted inputs including unknown keys unchanged exactly once without coercion or defaults, and preserves the unknown-tool exception.
- Static schemas compile at registration, an uncompilable schema throws an error naming the tool at registration, and subsequent executions reuse the compiled schema across ten calls.
- Function-valued definitions resolve on each execution and validate against that call'"'s schema, compiling new schema objects and reusing the validator for an already compiled schema object.

## Integration and migration

The rejection result follows the existing tool-result path back to the provider; the existing five-round bound limits retries. The execute doc comment points here for the rejection-versus-unknown-name contract. Executor failures remain executor failures.

The implementation also owes the runtime validator dependency, the README and adding-a-tool guide updates, the repo-level spec update, a consumer-facing changelog entry, a MINOR version bump and a PR migration note: executors no longer receive schema-invalid input, including executors that previously returned their own errors for it.
